\paragraph{Why minimum intervention works.}
In the 38.2\% of batches where $\aeff$ determines $\bstar$, the selected exponent is 4.9$\times$ lower than the ESS-only upper bound (\S\ref{sec:regime}); this rate is robust across contamination levels (33.4\% at $\rho{=}0.6$) and seeds (CV ${<}2\%$).
The aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL no better than uniform (17.693 vs.\ 17.694), confirming per-batch adaptation is essential: the method's value lies in applying different exponents to batches with different contamination compositions, not in finding a single good exponent.

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Why minimum intervention works.}
The $\aeff$ quality constraint prevents 4.9$\times$ over-aggressive downweighting in 38.2\% of batches (\S\ref{sec:regime}), robust across contamination levels (33.4\% at $\rho{=}0.6$) and seeds (CV ${<}2\%$).
Using the aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL comparable to uniform (17.693 vs.\ 17.694), confirming per-batch adaptation is essential.

\paragraph{PPL vs.\ downstream divergence.}
Downstream benchmarks at 0.6B show 4 of 5 tasks with small negative $\Delta$ ($p{\approx}0.19$, $N{=}5$, single seed); cross-model evaluation (Llama-3.2-3B, single seed) is similarly mixed (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream},~\ref{app:cross_model}).
The PPL gain may reflect distribution matching rather than generalizable quality improvement.

